# Canonical bytes must survive a round trip through Git unchanged. `vela replay`
# re-derives every root from the checked-out bytes, so a checkout filter,
# keyword expansion, working-tree encoding, or semantic merge driver that
# rewrote them would turn a clean clone into a failed verification. A local
# .git/info/attributes still outranks this file; this is a repository contract,
# not an enforcement mechanism.
#
# `vela init` scaffolds a leading `* text=auto eol=lf`. It is omitted here
# because this repository already has history: a global normalization rule takes
# effect on the next checkout across every tracked file, not only the ones named
# below, and the resulting diff would swamp whatever change it arrived with.
#
# The machine-written surfaces are `-text` rather than the scaffold's
# `text eol=lf`. records/ takes content-addressed blobs whose filename is their
# own digest, and end-of-line normalization is a byte rewrite; a sibling
# Frontier already stores a gzip stream there that `text eol=lf` would strip six
# CR bytes out of, breaking its own name. Nothing under records/ here is binary
# yet, so this rule is a guard against the day one arrives.
.vela/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
records/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
artifacts/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
frontier.toml -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge diff text eol=lf
targets.json -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge diff text eol=lf
